This is where pancake stands: what runs today, where the edges are, and where the
project is going. The code is the truth about the present; the documents linked
below argue the direction without pretending unfinished work has landed.

```=html
<nav class="cards" aria-label="Project status">
	<a class="card" href="https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/shipped.md">
		<h3>What ships today</h3>
		<p>The gated inventory of implemented behavior: the wire operation surface, both codecs, three netlayers, the actor layer, third-party handoffs, and live Goblins interop from every binding. A claim that stops matching the tree is treated as a bug.</p>
	</a>
	<a class="card" href="https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/boundaries.md">
		<h3>Where the edges are</h3>
		<p>Where the implemented surface ends, plainly: transport is cleartext TCP with encryption riding the separate iroh artifact, bootstrap registrations live for the process, and the OCapN Null (the spec's placeholder value) fails closed.</p>
	</a>
	<a class="card" href="https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/vision.md">
		<h3>Where it is going</h3>
		<p>The goal is to be the most performant OCapN implementation there is, fully canonical and proven by interop, with performance held to a measured contract rather than asserted.</p>
	</a>
	<a class="card" href="https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/decisions/README.md">
		<h3>Why the boundaries sit here</h3>
		<p>The accepted decision records: the netlayer contract, the one-parser framing, the C ABI allocation context, the iroh sidecar, and the rest. When a boundary moves, a superseding record explains the move.</p>
	</a>
</nav>
```

Two more documents carry the route in detail: the
[ladder](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/ladder.md) is the
ordered climb to 1.0 with each rung's standing, and the
[roadmap](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/roadmap.md) is the
concrete remaining work. Each claim in the shipped inventory is held by a gate, a
scenario, or a live interop run against
[Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/).
